# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows converts to CRLF on checkout by default. For most files that
# is harmless, but a few are read by Linux inside a container, where a stray
# carriage return is not whitespace:
#
#   - a shell script with CRLF fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M"
#   - an LDIF with CRLF imports as empty, which looks exactly like a wrong
#     base DN and costs an afternoon to find
#
# So those are pinned to LF regardless of platform or user configuration.

* text=auto

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.ldif      text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# And the same files with a suffix on the end, which the globs above do not
# match: deploy/ldap holds a slapd.conf.template and a structure.ldif.template,
# both substituted and then read by the same Linux tools as the originals. They
# fell through to text=auto and would have been checked out with CRLF - which is
# the LDIF failure described above, arriving by the one route the rule against it
# did not cover.
*.template  text eol=lf

# Go tooling writes and expects LF everywhere.
*.go        text eol=lf
go.mod      text eol=lf
go.sum      text eol=lf

# Read by golangci-lint-action, which wants a bare version and nothing else on
# the line. A trailing carriage return would be part of the version it looks up.
.golangci-lint-version text eol=lf

# Read by the process itself, and by GoFr's parser rather than by a shell: a
# trailing carriage return becomes part of the value, so LOG_LEVEL=INFO would be
# read as "INFO\r". The image is built from whatever checkout the build context
# came from, so pinning these is what keeps that from depending on whose machine
# built it - and configs/.env is exactly what the release notes tell somebody to
# create from deploy/.env.binary.example.
*.env       text eol=lf
.env.example      text eol=lf
.env.binary.example text eol=lf

# The embedded web assets are served byte for byte; CRLF would only inflate
# them and make diffs noisier.
*.js        text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf

# And the asset the rules above still missed, by the same suffix trap the
# templates were added for: the shipped mark is an SVG, which is text, embedded
# with go:embed and served byte for byte - and *.svg matched none of the globs
# above. So a build on Windows put it in the binary with CRLF while the same
# commit built on the Linux runner baked in LF, and the two binaries served
# different bytes, and different ETags, for one unchanged file.
*.svg       text eol=lf

# A substituted file again, and again a suffix the rule for its own type does
# not reach: build/versioninfo.json.in is read by sed inside
# build/windows-resource.sh, and the JSON that comes out is handed to
# goversioninfo. *.json does not match a name ending in .json.in, exactly as
# *.conf did not match slapd.conf.template.
*.in        text eol=lf

# The icons are binary. Git works that out on its own from the NUL bytes in
# their headers and would leave them alone regardless - but a byte-for-byte
# served asset is not where that detection should be load-bearing, and saying
# so costs one line.
*.ico       binary

# Git's own two files, pinned for the sake of the check rather than for anything
# that reads them. `git ls-files --eol` is how a wrong line ending gets found
# here, and it is only worth running while every row reads w/lf: two permanent
# exceptions teach whoever runs it to skim past the output, which is the state
# this whole file exists to avoid.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
